= Next steps: future research directions
The next research program should test whether Keating produces durable understanding with less instructional repair. The creator study provides the starting cases, subject areas, and failure examples. Five questions organize the next stage.

== What does the creator retain and transfer?
Follow a preregistered set of new learning topics prospectively. Collect a precheck, an immediate parallel-form assessment, unaided recall after seven and 21 days, and a new application problem. Use seven-day reconstruction as the primary outcome; retain the creator's actual explanations and score them against a fixed rubric. Record assistance and intervening practice. These proposed follow-ups extend the distinction between immediate performance and later learning examined in retrieval and AI-tutoring research @roediger2006; @bastani2025.

== Which improvements come from Keating, and which from the model?
Compare a frozen baseline teaching revision with a revised one across recorded model configurations. Randomize comparable new topic blocks within subject and prior-knowledge strata; keep the assigned configuration fixed during each lesson. Use different learning material across conditions to avoid treating a previously taught concept as new. Score assessments without revealing the teaching condition. Report revision effects, model effects, and their interaction, alongside inference cost and response latency. This directly tests the model-and-skill relationship raised by WikiSkill @tang2026wikiskill.

== How much work does the learner do to keep the teacher useful?
Record requested repairs, missing artifacts, grading disputes, and time spent restoring a working lesson. Distinguish productive conceptual disagreement from operating the interface or correcting faulty reference material. Compare repair burden alongside understanding: a successful revision should help the learner reason while reducing avoidable maintenance. Pair the event record with short creator reflections on why each intervention was needed. This develops the autobiographical-design contribution into an account of how learning and system development shape one another @neustaedter2012.

== Can the evaluator recognize good teaching and consequential errors?
Build an independently rated calibration set from checked examples of conceptual correction, plausible guessing, premature praise, faulty code, and failed interactions. Compare candidate judge models with human ratings; report false mastery judgments, missed factual errors, agreement, and missing-score rates. Swap answer order in paired comparisons and use executable references for programming examples @zheng2023. Reserve new subject and problem families for evaluation, and renew the holdout before testing another revision against it.

== Does the teaching work for people who did not build it?
Recruit learners with varied prior knowledge and compare Keating with the same underlying model in a basic chat interface and with appropriate existing learning materials. Stratify by subject and baseline knowledge, size the study from pilot variability and a declared minimum meaningful effect, and preregister its outcomes. Measure delayed understanding, transfer, completion, and repair burden. This extends structured-tutoring comparisons @kestin2025 and reveals which benefits depend on the creator's expertise and persistence.

The sequence is practical: establish the prospective record and calibrated assessments, run the revision-by-model pilot, then conduct the independent-learner study. Publish the protocol, scoring rubrics, versioned configurations, and aggregate outcomes so each claimed improvement has a traceable test.
